<div style="background-color: #1a5276; padding: 20px; text-align: center; color: white;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Celebrations and Traditions</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Celebrate This Tradition Because...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Show recognition of specific details when listening to explanations. Interactive: Contribute to simple discussions on a variety of issues.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow audio narratives about Panamanian cultural events and show recognition by answering questions about key details (e.g., "Los Diablos wear masks during the Festival de la Pollera Congo because they represent spirits."). Can contribute to simple discussions about celebrations and customs in their community by sharing personal experiences and responding to peers' questions (e.g., "What do people typically do during Fiestas Patrias?" "They participate in parades, eat traditional food, and enjoy live music. My family has attended the celebrations for many years, and last year, we saw the parade."). Can follow a short story about a cultural tradition and recall main details (e.g., "People celebrate with parades.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2; border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>

    <!-- STAGE 1: Warm-up / Pre-task -->
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification. Activating prior knowledge and introducing key vocabulary for listening comprehension.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays 3-4 vivid images of Panamanian celebrations (e.g., Fiestas Patrias parade, Festival de la Mejorana, a traditional dance). Students listen and identify what they see. The teacher asks, "What do you see in these pictures?" and "What celebration is this?" The teacher introduces key vocabulary words from the images: <i>parade, celebration, dance, mask, festival, tradition</i>. Students repeat the words after the teacher.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes the new vocabulary on the board and models the pronunciation, paying attention to word stress (e.g., "ce-le-BRA-tion," "TRA-di-tion," "FES-ti-val," "MASK"). The teacher pronounces sentences like, "Panama celebrates its independence with a big <i>parade</i>." "Many communities have a local <i>festival</i> with traditional <i>dance</i>." Students listen and repeat chorally and individually.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the vocabulary and concepts.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a <i>parade</i> a quiet or noisy event?" (Expected answer: Noisy!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do people wear special clothes for a <i>celebration</i>?" (Expected answer: Yes, sometimes traditional clothes!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a <i>mask</i> something you wear on your hands or your face?" (Expected answer: On your face!)</li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
    </tr>

    <!-- STAGE 2: Presentation -->
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to "Celebrations and Traditions." Focused listening for gist and specific information.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio narrative (1-1.5 minutes) about a Panamanian cultural tradition (e.g., "Fiestas Patrias" or "Carnaval"). The narrative should be clear, slightly slowed down for A2.3, and naturally incorporate 8-10 target vocabulary words: <i>tradition, custom, independence, parade, celebration, mask, dance, festival, national, enjoy</i>. It will also use present simple and past simple for descriptions ("Panama celebrates...", "Panama became independent...").</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interpret meaning from audio materials about cultural events. Show recognition of specific details when listening to explanations.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question (First listen):</b> The teacher plays the audio once. Students listen for the main idea. "What is the audio mainly about? A) Sports in Panama, B) Panamanian traditions, C) Famous Panamanian food." (Expected answer: B)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Second listen):</b> The teacher plays the audio again. Students listen and decide if statements are True (T) or False (F).
                    <ul style="margin-top: 4px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Panama celebrates its independence in November." (T)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"People wear masks only during Carnival." (F - could be other festivals too, depending on audio)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Traditional dances are an important part of the celebration." (T)</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">12 min</td>
    </tr>

    <!-- STAGE 3: Preparation / Practice -->
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening. Controlled and semi-controlled activities to reinforce vocabulary, grammar, and pronunciation from the audio input.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening for specific details and practicing pronunciation of target vocabulary and third-person singular verb endings. Grammar focus: Present simple for traditions ("Panama celebrates Fiestas Patrias..."), gerund after prepositions ("excited about celebrating..."). Vocabulary: <i>tradition, celebration, parade, dance, mask, festival, heritage, custom, national, enjoy, gather</i>.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Gap-fill Listening (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher provides a transcript of a short section of the audio with blanks for 4-5 key vocabulary words (e.g., "During Fiestas Patrias, people often watch a grand ______ in the streets."). Students listen to the audio section again and write the missing words. Teacher checks answers as a class.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Listen and Repeat (Pronunciation Drill):</b> The teacher focuses on specific words from the audio.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Word Stress:</b> The teacher says words like "cultural," "celebration," "gather," "mask," emphasizing correct stress. Students listen and repeat.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Third-person -s/-es/-ies:</b> The teacher plays sentences from the audio containing verbs with third-person singular -s endings. "Panama <i>celebrates</i>..." (/s/), "The community <i>gathers</i>..." (/z/), "Everyone <i>wishes</i>..." (/ɪz/). Students listen carefully to the sounds and repeat the verbs.</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Listen and Choose (Multiple Choice):</b> The teacher reads aloud 3-4 short descriptions of different traditions, each using target vocabulary. For each description, students listen and choose the best matching image (from a handout or projected images) or the correct adjective to describe it (e.g., "Is this tradition A) national, B) local, or C) cultural?"). Students can work in pairs to discuss their choices before a class check.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
    </tr>

    <!-- STAGE 4: Performance / Production -->
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Celebrations and Traditions." Focus on fluency and collaborative interaction through listening and responding.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Pair-share and interactive listening. Students work in pairs to discuss a tradition. One student describes a tradition (either from the audio or one they know from their community), and the other student listens attentively and asks questions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher gives each student a prompt card (e.g., "Describe a tradition you celebrate" or "Describe a tradition from the audio you just heard").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Student A:</b> Describes a tradition for 30-45 seconds, using at least 3-4 target vocabulary words (e.g., "My family <i>celebrates</i> Christmas. It's a very old <i>tradition</i>. We <i>gather</i> together and <i>enjoy</i> special food."). They can also use present perfect: "We <i>have participated</i> in these celebrations for many years."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Student B:</b> Listens carefully to Student A's description. After Student A finishes, Student B asks two follow-up questions to get more details (e.g., "What do people typically do during that <i>celebration</i>?" "Why is that <i>tradition</i> important to your family?").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students then switch roles.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, facilitating and monitoring listening comprehension and interaction, noting use of target vocabulary and correct responses.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
    </tr>

    <!-- STAGE 5: Assessment / Post-task -->
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-reflection.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment:</b> The teacher uses an observation checklist during the Performance stage, focusing on:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listening Comprehension:</b> Did Student B ask relevant follow-up questions, showing they understood Student A's description?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Did students incorporate at least 2-3 target words when describing or asking questions?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> Was their pronunciation of target words clear?</li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher calls on a few students to briefly share one interesting detail they *heard* from their partner's description (e.g., "I learned that [Partner's name]'s family enjoys a big dinner during their Christmas <i>celebration</i>.")</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students write their answers to the following questions on a small slip of paper:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new Panamanian tradition or detail you *heard* about today that you found interesting?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new vocabulary word you learned and understood today?"</li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
    </tr>

    <!-- STAGE 6: Reflection -->
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review, consolidation, and forward planning.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel about understanding details when you listen to explanations about traditions?" (Rate 1-5, 5 being very confident)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of listening today, and how can you improve next time?"</li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson to enhance listening comprehension and vocabulary acquisition? (e.g., The use of visual aids during warm-up, repetition of vocabulary, clear audio.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson based on student performance today? (e.g., Some students struggled with the pace of the audio; perhaps a pre-listening vocabulary match will help. More explicit pronunciation practice of '-s' endings.)</li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 2, students will read a text about a Panamanian tradition, reinforcing the same vocabulary words introduced today and understanding them in a new context, building on their listening comprehension.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3 min</td>
    </tr>
</table>